<Layout title="Saturno — Gabriel Almir" description="My public lab for exploring work organization without promising what the code cannot prove." lang="en" alternateHref="/projects/saturno"><Shell locale="en"><main id="main" class="case-page"><header class="case-hero"><p class="kicker">Public project / 2025</p><h1>Saturno</h1><p>I built Saturno as a public lab for exploring task and project organization.</p></header><section class="case-facts"><article><small>Constraint</small><h2>Learn without inflating the story.</h2><p>I wanted to experiment with a work tool without presenting intent as adoption, scale, or a proven result.</p></article><article><small>Decision</small><h2>Keep evidence close to the code.</h2><p>I recorded scope locally and limited this account to what the public project demonstrates. I make no messaging or distributed architecture claims for Saturno.</p></article><article><small>Status</small><h2>A lab, not a finished product.</h2><p>TypeScript, React, Laravel, PHP, and PostgreSQL appear in the project. Its learning and limits matter more than a stack inventory.</p></article></section><section class="case-note"><p class="kicker">What stayed with me</p><h2>Public projects also need to say “not yet.”</h2><p>Treating a hypothesis as a hypothesis makes the work more honest and leaves room for the next piece of evidence.</p><a class="button ink" href="https://github.com/gabrielalmir/saturno">View public code ↗</a></section></main></Shell></Layout>
